{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_012QKYneYVyWAjye1Mp7DKwY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! The C library consists of a single translation unit (`src/lib.c`) whose only\n4\t//! public symbol is `pow43` (declared in `include/lib.h` as\n5\t//! `float pow43(int x);`). There are no namespace/renaming macros in the public\n6\t//! header, so the exported linker symbol is plainly `pow43`.\n7\t//!\n8\t//! The original C:\n9\t//!\n10\t//! ```c\n11\t//! static const float g_pow43[129 + 16] = { ... };\n12\t//!\n13\t//! float pow43(int x) {\n14\t//!     float frac;\n15\t//!     int sign, mult = 256;\n16\t//!     if (x < 129) {\n17\t//!         return g_pow43[16 + x];\n18\t//!     }\n19\t//!     if (x < 1024) {\n20\t//!         mult = 16;\n21\t//!         x <<= 3;\n22\t//!     }\n23\t//!     sign = 2 * x & 64;\n24\t//!     frac = (float)((x & 63) - sign) / ((x & ~63) + sign);\n25\t//!     return g_pow43[16 + ((x + sign) >> 6)] *\n26\t//!            (1.f + frac * ((4.f / 3) + frac * (2.f / 9))) * mult;\n27\t//! }\n28\t//! ```\n29\t//!\n30\t//! Bug-for-bug notes (behaviour deliberately preserved, not \"fixed\"):\n31\t//!\n32\t//! * The table lookup is *not* range checked. `x` in `-16..=128` indexes the\n33\t//!   table in bounds, but any `x < -16` (and, on the second path, any `x`\n34\t//!   large enough that `16 + ((x + sign) >> 6)` exceeds 144) performs an\n35\t//!   out-of-bounds read exactly as the C does. This is reproduced with raw\n36\t//!   pointer arithmetic so that no bounds-check panic is introduced where the\n37\t//!   C would simply read adjacent memory.\n38\t//! * `2 * x`, `x << 3` and `x + sign` are signed `int` operations that can\n39\t//!   overflow for extreme inputs; the C relies on the usual two's-complement\n40\t//!   wrap-around of the target, which is reproduced with wrapping operators\n41\t//!   instead of Rust's panicking/UB-free-but-different arithmetic.\n42\t//! * All arithmetic is performed in single precision (`f32`), matching C's\n43\t//!   `float` semantics on x86-64 (`FLT_EVAL_METHOD == 0`), including the\n44\t//!   left-to-right association of the final multiplication chain.\n45\t\n46\t#![allow(non_upper_case_globals)]\n47\t\n48\tuse std::ffi::c_int;\n49\t\n50\t/// `static const float g_pow43[129 + 16]` from `c_src/src/lib.c`.\n51\t///\n52\t/// 145 entries: the first 16 hold the (negative) values used when `pow43` is\n53\t/// called with a small negative argument, entry 16 onwards hold `x^(4/3)` for\n54\t/// `x = 0 ..= 128`.\n55\tstatic g_pow43: [f32; 129 + 16] = [\n56\t    0.0f32, -1.0f32, -2.519842f32, -4.326749f32, -6.349604f32,\n57\t    -8.549880f32, -10.902724f32, -13.390518f32, -16.000000f32, -18.720754f32,\n58\t    -21.544347f32, -24.463781f32, -27.473142f32, -30.567351f32, -33.741992f32,\n59\t    -36.993181f32, 0.0f32, 1.0f32, 2.519842f32, 4.326749f32,\n60\t    6.349604f32, 8.549880f32, 10.902724f32, 13.390518f32, 16.000000f32,\n61\t    18.720754f32, 21.544347f32, 24.463781f32, 27.473142f32, 30.567351f32,\n62\t    33.741992f32, 36.993181f32, 40.317474f32, 43.711787f32, 47.173345f32,\n63\t    50.699631f32, 54.288352f32, 57.937408f32, 61.644865f32, 65.408941f32,\n64\t    69.227979f32, 73.100443f32, 77.024898f32, 81.000000f32, 85.024491f32,\n65\t    89.097188f32, 93.216975f32, 97.382800f32, 101.593667f32, 105.848633f32,\n66\t    110.146801f32, 114.487321f32, 118.869381f32, 123.292209f32, 127.755065f32,\n67\t    132.257246f32, 136.798076f32, 141.376907f32, 145.993119f32, 150.646117f32,\n68\t    155.335327f32, 160.060199f32, 164.820202f32, 169.614826f32, 174.443577f32,\n69\t    179.305980f32, 184.201575f32, 189.129918f32, 194.090580f32, 199.083145f32,\n70\t    204.107210f32, 209.162385f32, 214.248292f32, 219.364564f32, 224.510845f32,\n71\t    229.686789f32, 234.892058f32, 240.126328f32, 245.389280f32, 250.680604f32,\n72\t    256.000000f32, 261.347174f32, 266.721841f32, 272.123723f32, 277.552547