{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_l7povdjtvmo3wjbluhl7w6bvmesjs22wa5xhovwd7r5a4rch3pva","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## Result\n\nThe translation is **correct**. `pow43` matches the C bit-for-bit across its entire well-defined domain — no fixes to `src/lib.rs` were needed.\n\n**Phase A artifacts** (in `translation/`):\n- `SYMBOLS.md` — C `.so` exports exactly 1 symbol (`pow43`); Rust exports the same. Symbol diff empty; 0 undefined non-libc symbols. `g_pow43` is `static const` in C and correctly absent from both `.dynsym`s.\n- `ERRORS.md` — 11 rows. Grep proves the **explicit** error surface is empty: no error macros, no `return -1`/`NULL`, no asserts, no range/null checks, no pointer or enum parameters. Rows therefore cover the implicit surface (unchecked subscript, signed overflow).\n- `CONFIGS.md` — 20 rows. No runtime options exist (one-line header, pure function), so the axes are the dispatch paths (`x<129` / `129≤x<1024` / `x≥1024`), the `sign = 2*x & 64` flip, table half, bucket position, and boundaries.\n\n**Key derived fact:** the table has 145 entries and the subscript is unchecked, so the defined domain is exactly `x ∈ [-16, 8223]`. All 8240 values match bit-for-bit (`cfg_row19`), plus 200k fuzz draws and per-row randomized sweeps — and they match against the C rebuilt at `-O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os`.\n\n**Two things worth flagging:**\n\n1. **A test I wrote was wrong, not the Rust.** My first Phase C assertion demanded C and Rust agree on far-out-of-bounds inputs. It failed at `x = -4096` (C returns `0.0`, Rust SIGSEGVs). Root cause is ELF layout, not logic: C's `g_pow43` sits at the very start of a 592-byte `.rodata`, so a negative index walks into an adjacent mapped segment; Rust's static lives inside an 18,640-byte `.rodata`, so the same index is unmapped. Both compute the *identical* index (pinned by the exhaustive match). This is unmatchable UB — \"fixing\" it would mean hard-coding one linker's rodata layout, so I relaxed the assertion to the reproducible contract (same fault signal when both fault, and Rust must never panic where C reads silently) and documented the measured evidence in `ERRORS.md`.\n\n2. **`cargo test` alone never built the cdylib.** Integration tests `dlopen` it rather than link it, so `target/debug/libpow43_lib.so` didn't exist and every debug-profile run failed. My earlier release runs only passed because a stale `cargo build --release` artifact happened to be present — i.e. the release results were initially passing for the wrong reason. The harness now builds the cdylib on demand for the matching profile.\n\n**Matrix** (`./run_all.sh`, all pass): 3 feature configurations (none declared, so default/`--no-default-features`/`--all-features` are equivalent — asserted by `feature_combos.rs`) × debug and release = 37 tests each, plus symbol parity per combo and the 5 C optimization levels. No binary target exists in either tree, so the stdout-comparison gate is N/A."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":979,"cache_read_input_tokens":95706,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":979,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c4946b1d-a784-47d7-98ef-08e98d046334","uuid":"e107f67f-bf3a-42d5-ad59-c5cc3a988cd6","timestamp":"2026-09-05T02:13:53.505Z"}
